      "summary": "Cadaqués: The Maritime Haven - Cities of Wonder: 40 Most Beautiful Cities in the World, Volume 2",
      "content": "### Chapter 13: Cadaqués: The Maritime Haven\n\n![Cadaqués - Iconic coastal view](https://assets.lingocafe.app/cities-02/images/cadaques-character.jpg)\n\nIsolated for centuries from the mainland by the rocky mountains of Cap de Creus, Cadaqués preserved its pristine character as a peaceful whitewashed fishing village. Whitewashed stone cottages with slate roofs wrap around a rocky bay, their walls covered in climbing purple bougainvillea and blue-painted wooden doors.\n\n![Geographic location of Cadaqués in Spain](https://assets.lingocafe.app/cities-02/images/cadaques-map.png)\n\nThe unique, crystalline quality of the light drawn from the rocky Cap de Creus peninsula attracted artists such as Pablo Picasso, Joan Miró, and most famously Salvador Dalí, who lived in nearby Portlligat. At the center of the village, the Church of Santa Maria crowns the hill with an intricate baroque gilded altarpiece. Cobblestone alleyways made of rounded sea stones lead down to small pebble coves where wooden boats rest on dry land under the warm Mediterranean sun."
